NAME
       mount, umount, mount9 - attach and detach filesystems

SYNOPSIS
       #include <sys/cervus.h>

       int cervus_disk_mount(const char *dev, const char *path);
       int cervus_disk_umount(const char *path);
       int cervus_mount9(const char *host, int port, const char *path);

DESCRIPTION
       Mounts a block device, detecting the filesystem from its
       superblock. ext2, ext4, FAT32, ISO 9660 and UDF are recognised;
       ext4 is read through its extent tree, and UDF is read and
       written without the usual write-once restriction.

       mount9 attaches a remote 9P server over the network instead of a
       block device.

       Unmounting flushes pending writes first, so a device removed
       after umount returns is consistent.

RETURN VALUE
       0 on success, negative on failure.

ERRORS
       ENODEV     no such device
       ENOTBLK    the device holds no filesystem this kernel knows
       EBUSY      the mount point is in use

SEE ALSO
       mount(1), mount9(1), statvfs(2), disk(2)
